This being my second ascension (first one was Thvor the Barbarian; feel free
to look that one up, if you want), I'm still playing very cautiously, and so
felt no qualms about burning through wishes as fast as I wanted to, or
genociding freely, etc., so I'm afraid this was a fairly boring ascension.
That said:

I've tried the protection racket many, many times, and generally either die
painfully, or reach minetown with very little money and a pet who doesn't
seem to want to help clean out the shops for more.  So I decided to forget
that approach for a while, and just started whacking things with my pickaxe.
I did let my kitten get a fair number of kills, and so had a large cat by
the time I reached town, but he died shortly thereafter.  Several other pets
along the way failed to last very long.

There was a general store on the second level with an expensive lamp, boots
which turned out to be water walking, and a wand of polymorph, which I
claimed around the time I went on the quest.  The mine entrance was on 4;
deeper than I'm used to seeing it.  Made me glad I wasn't trying the racket.
There was a weapons shop on 4 as well, but the only interesting thing it had
was a pair of jumping boots.  Which I never used.  There was also an altar
on 4, which quickly became lawful.  I liked that level.  :-)  Minetown had a
small general store, a deli, and a cross-aligned temple; Izchak was very
obliging, and sold me another expensive lamp.  Sokoban yielded a BoH, which
was slightly annoying, since I already had two.  Mines end was the mimic
variant; being an Arc made finding the right gray stone very easy.  :-)

Headed back to level 4, made some holy water, and turned one of my lamps
into gray dragon scale mail.  (I wanted to make sure my luck was positive
before I did this, since Thvor had ended up with cursed mail from an early
wish.)

Had some problems getting intrinsics early on--I didn't get telepathy until
18180!  I'd found a ring of teleport control, though, so after a little bit
of thought, went ahead and munched on a nymph corpse.  This was my first
experience with that combination, and I have to say--I think it's
over-rated.  Most of the time my teleportitis kicked in, I was either a)
carefully exploring a level, and thus not wanting to jump very far, or b) on
a non-teleport level.  Sure, it was nice when it did help me across a level,
but that was rare.

The quest went very smoothly--as I posted before, I snuck up on the minion
of Huehotl, hit him once with my (wished-for, from a throne) Grayswandir; he
immediately teleported away, leaving the Orb behind.  After I grabbed the
Orb, he teleported back to me to give me the Bell as well.  Once I was back
on the quest home level, I tried poly-piling stuff, which resulted in some
nice equipment, including my first robe.  Which I promptly fire-proofed, and
then evaporated.  (Lesson learned: don't keep enchanting until it
vibrates--that's one step too far!)  I also got some speed boots, which also
became fixed, and evaporated.  (Dangit!  Now, lesson learned!  Really!)

So, muttering curses under my breath, I headed down, crossed Medusa's (used
a blindfold), and made it to the castle.  Found the tune with my magic harp;
really, who would use 'eebee' as a pass tune?  Don't these people want
something difficult to discover?  The wand, as I'd indicated at the
beginning, was shamelessly abused, replacing my boots of speed, my robe, and
delivering me gauntlets of power.  I probably should have gone with
dexterity, but Thvor's barbarian-ness was still lingering, I think.  :-)  I
also wished up an amulet of reflection, which was perhaps a bit
extravagant--I'd gotten silver scales and a polished shield in Ludious, but
I was having fun two-weaponing my Grayswandir and another silver sabre.
(That combination, at least, is not over-rated.  Having done it once,
though, I think I'll avoid it in the future...)  Two more wishes eventually
became magic markers, allowing me to blessed-genocide L and R; later, I went
ahead and bumped off all ; as well, more for the heck of it than from any
immediate threat.  Like I said, I wasn't holding back for this one.

Gehennom went very smoothly, except for my missing the stairs to Vlad's on
the way down.  It helped that I'd learned magic mapping before I came in,
and could cast it at about 40% failure, so my standard practice was to map
the level, cast light to mark the upstairs clearly, and then just dig for
the stairs down.  Orcus surprised me by not trying to zap his wand at all,
despite his getting several turns in which he could have; of course, I
didn't zap his wand, either, after I'd pulled it from his dead fingers,
having picked one up in Ludious.  I did get sucked by a mind-flayer, which
made me feel stupid (in real life, as well as in the game)--I'd thought that
cancelling it would prevent that attack, but, well, it didn't.  So I ended
up genociding those as well.

When I made it to the sanctum level, I was about halfway through clearing
out the graveyard before it occured to me that I didn't need to--I wasn't
coming back that way, and the undead were resting peacefully, so I just
snuck by the rest of them.  Plowed past most of the priests, zapped a wand
of secret door detection I'd brought along for that express purpose, and
went on in.  Beat the high priest down, grabbed the amulet, and pulled out
my cursed !oGL--I was on my way out, and nothing was going to stop me!

...not that much of anything tried, honestly.  The mysterious force pulled
me from 43 to 44, then 43 to 45, then 36 to 37, then 35 to 37--I was
expecting much worse than that.  The wizard showed up a few more times, but
didn't stick around much when he did.  I did run into a small problem,
though, in that I'd forgotten several level layouts (that was one vicious
suck, I must say!), so I had to do some searching at times; the light I'd
cast around most of the upstairs helped while in Gehennom, though it didn't
help in the main Dungeon.  Oops.

Before leaving, I dropped much of my unnecessary junk (zapped it with a
/oCancellation to make sure no one used it against me before I left), and
wished up a ring of conflict, having found it immensely useful on my first
ascension.  Didn't put it on until I was on air, and took it off before
leaving water; after getting bogged down on astral, I put it back on--better
than snapping /oTeleport, if you ask me.  On astral, my first step was, of
course, to put on my blindfold to find out where the three amigos were.
Famine's to the right, Death's in the center, so I headed over to talk with
Pestilence on the left.  Managed to get to the altar; lo and behold, it's
lawful.  After clearing the wanna-be heros out of the room (ok, I killed
them), I knelt down and #offered.

